description:
MIP-3 section 9. The daemon speaks protobuf, length-prefixed, over a Unix socket, with the schema in `proto/` beside `muster.proto`, generated by the same toolchain, in a `muster-daemon-proto` crate shared by the daemon and its clients.

What the MIP fixes:
- three connections to one socket, each opening with a version handshake: control (requests, answers, events), input (unacknowledged input events, never queued behind a large answer), stream (one per bridge: replay, bytes, credit);
- answers say done / already so / refused-not-there / refused-with-reason;
- every event carries a sequence number; subscribe returns a snapshot and the sequence it is current to, then every later event in order, nothing replayed from before; the events a mutation produced are delivered before its answer, and the answer names the last of them;
- agent state for every pane on the one event stream;
- Muster's tab and pane names on the wire, minted by Muster and passed in the creating request;
- requests are namespaced by service (`pane.*`, `tab.*`, `session.*`) so a later message service takes its own;
- first-version requests: snapshot/subscribe; create a pane beside another (four sides) or in a new tab, with ratio, grid in cells and pixels, cwd, env, command, name; close pane/tab; resize, zoom, swap, move; set split ratio; rename pane/tab; paged read by absolute row; set palette, shell, scrollback depth; send manifests; stop. No focus request.

Worth deciding here and recording: how app/daemon version skew is expressed (the app adopts a running daemon whose protocol it supports), and how the schema is checked for compatibility across versions - the herdr api-schema drift check in `./dev` has no successor yet, and a remote daemon adopted across an app upgrade is exactly the case it would guard.

For context on what the client side must eventually cover, `crates/muster-herdr/src/intent.rs` `request()` (around line 932) maps every `BackendIntent` onto herdr today.
